@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
title Browser viewport — watching the agent's Chromium from the host, across the sandbox boundary

' purpose: why the host cannot reach the sandbox's browser directly, and the two ports that fix it
' covers: internal/sbx/run.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go
' covers: internal/run/run.go
' covers: e2e/browser_viewport_test.go
'
' ── TWO PORTS, BECAUSE ONE CANNOT DO BOTH JOBS ─────────────────────────────
' Chromium's DevTools endpoint refuses every peer that is not loopback: bound
' with --remote-debugging-address 0.0.0.0 and asked from the sandbox's OWN
' address it answers nothing (measured 2026-09-02 — loopback 200, sandbox IP
' reset), which is exactly how sbx's port forwarder arrives. So Chromium stays
' on loopback and a RELAY owns the published port, connecting onward as a
' loopback client.
'
' BrowserCDPArgs is the AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS value that makes the agent's own
' Chromium expose CDP, preserving whatever the operator already set.
' agent-browser launches Chromium itself and picks its own endpoint, so there
' was nothing to attach to; --args is its documented way to pass browser flags
' through, and a fixed port there is what turns the agent's browser into one the
' host can find. Measured against agent-browser 0.36.0.
'
' The relay runs in the FOREGROUND so the run owns its lifetime: when proveo
' stops waiting the relay goes with it, rather than outliving the session as an
' orphan holding a published port. It passes `-w /` for the reason the state
' sync does — the container WorkingDir can stop resolving mid-run, and an exec
' that inherits it dies at chdir.
'
' ── WHY THE HOST STARTS IT, AND WHY IT CHECKS Running ──────────────────────
' Started from the host rather than seeded into the image for two reasons: the
' exec's lifetime is the run's, so the relay cannot outlive the session; and an
' already-built -browser image gains the viewport without a rebuild.
'
' Guarded on Running, never merely on Exists: `sbx exec` RESTARTS a stopped
' sandbox, so a retry loop that did not check would resurrect the VM after the
' agent had finished with it.
'
' The host port is reserved by binding it and letting it go, so the URL can be
' printed BEFORE the sandbox exists. Impure by nature, which is why it is the
' caller's job and not Spec's: a plan rendered by --print must not depend on
' which ports this machine happens to have free. The gap between releasing and
' sbx binding is a race nothing can close from here, and losing it costs the
' viewport, not the run.
'
' Every link measured 2026-09-02 against sbx (schema 1.132.0), Chromium 149.0.7827.0
' and agent-browser 0.36.0, on proveo/claudecode-browser.

actor "Operator" as OP
participant "proveo run\n(browser add-on ticked)" as RUN <<app>>
participant "sbx port forwarder\n-p <hostPort>:9222" as FWD <<cloud>>
box "sandbox microVM" #F4F9FB
  participant "relay\n0.0.0.0:9222 → 127.0.0.1:9223" as RELAY <<app>>
  participant "Chromium DevTools\n127.0.0.1:9223" as CDP <<app>>
  participant "agent-browser\n(the agent's own tool)" as AB <<app>>
end box

RUN ARROW_MAIN RUN : pick a free host port BEFORE launch,\nso the URL is printed ahead of the TUI
RUN ARROW_MAIN FWD : " ""sbx run -p <hostPort>:9222"" (creation-time) "
RUN ARROW_MAIN AB : " ""-e AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS=…,--remote-debugging-port=9223"" "
AB ARROW_MAIN CDP : launches Chromium on the PINNED port
RUN ARROW_QUEUE RELAY : " ""sbx exec -w / <sid> -- python3 -c <relay>"" \nheld open for the run's life "
OP ARROW_MAIN FWD : DevTools · Playwright ""connectOverCDP""\nhttp://127.0.0.1:<hostPort>
FWD ARROW_MAIN RELAY : forwarded connection\n(arrives from a NON-loopback peer)
RELAY ARROW_MAIN CDP : re-dialled as a loopback client
CDP ARROW_MAIN OP : target list · page WS URLs · a page it can drive
FWD ARROW_ERROR CDP : publishing 9223 directly — reset, always

note over CDP
  **Chromium's DevTools endpoint refuses every peer that is not loopback**, and
  that is the whole reason a relay exists. Measured from inside the sandbox, one
  process bound with ""--remote-debugging-address=0.0.0.0"":
  loopback answered **200**, its own address answered **nothing** (reset). sbx's
  forwarder arrives exactly the second way, so ""-p <host>:9223"" could never work
  no matter what the ""Host"" header said — an explicit ""Host: 127.0.0.1:9223"" was
  reset too. A plain HTTP server on the same sandbox, published the same way, was
  reachable throughout: the forwarder was never the problem.
end note

note over AB
  **agent-browser launches Chromium itself and picks its own endpoint**, so there
  was nothing to attach to. ""--args"" / ""AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS"" is its documented
  passthrough for browser flags, and a fixed ""--remote-debugging-port"" there is
  what turns the agent's browser into one the host can find. An operator who
  already pinned a port keeps theirs — two such flags and Chromium picks the
  loser.

  The env var travels as RUN environment, not as an image default: sbx launches
  the agent through its own kit and never runs the entrypoint, so an export
  there would reach the docker backend alone.
end note

note over RELAY
  **The relay is started from the HOST, not seeded into the image.** Its exec's
  lifetime is the run's, so it cannot outlive the session holding a published
  port; and an already-built ""-browser"" image gains the viewport with no rebuild.
  It is python3 stdlib passed as ""-c"", which the base image already carries.

  Restarts are guarded on ""Running"", never on ""Exists"": ""sbx exec"" RESTARTS a
  stopped sandbox, so a retry loop that only checked existence would resurrect
  the VM after the agent had finished with it.

  ""-w /"" for the reason ""SaveStateArgs"" pins it — the container WorkingDir can
  stop resolving mid-run (virtiofs-cwd-invalidation.puml) and an exec that
  inherits it dies at chdir.
end note

note over OP, RUN
  **This is the sbx answer to a question the host bridge cannot take.**
  ""claude-in-chrome"" points the OTHER way: the agent reaches OUT to the operator's
  own Chrome through a relay on the host, named as ""host.docker.internal"" — which
  only the docker backend's bridge network resolves, and which a sandbox VM
  cannot name at all. Published ports run host→sandbox, so they cannot serve it.

  Nothing leaves this machine: sbx binds published ports on loopback (127.0.0.1
  and ::1) only. The exposure is a CDP endpoint with full control of that
  browser, which is why it is scoped to the run and to the add-on that asked
  for it.
end note

@enduml
